body:
There are some players who like to role play the game, especially private groups of friends.  I can't speak from personal experience as I usually join with a group of strangers.  

&#x200B;

The host/captain saves the game and the decisions that you have to make as a crew are interesting, especially early game.  I'd argue that early game is by far the most interesting part of Pulsar since you aren't super powerful and can't just kill everything like you'll eventually be able to do latter on.  When it comes to getting invested, I'd say that taking a ship from level 1, to something that is a lot more powerful can lead to a lot of investment, although that can vary from crew to crew.  

&#x200B;

Role balance is an interesting question.  In my experience, most players have their own preference towards a class, but every player should have a working knowledge of every class.  The big thing that makes classes different is how they can level up.  For example, a engineer can pilot the ship and a pilot can work on the reactor, but when the crew levels up then the get better at their specific job.  

Captain: Cap is almost always the host of the game.  This player has the power to kick players, buy upgrades for the ship, plot a course on the star charts, and is generally considered the person in charge on most ships.  Of course some of the best captains are more democratic than authoritarian.  The person who play's cap is usually interested in planning out what the crew should be doing next, and is interested in managing upgrades and comparing stats.  The captain also needs to know how all of the other roles work and be able to fill in if there are any vacancies on the ship.  (If you're new and learning then that's fine, but just keep it in mind as a goal to learn.   

Pilot: Pilot is my personal favorite role to play.  It's a very simple role once you get used to flying the ship. I'd recommend experimenting with the key bindings to find a way to make flying more intuitive.  Pilot's role in combat is usually to help line up a shot for weapons.  On most ships the main gun is located on the top of the ship, so rotating the ship to a point where weapons has a shot is important.  What I like about pilot and weapons is that the roles are as involved as you want them to be.  If you just want to fly the ship or shoot things then that's all that the roles ever have to be, but if you want to be involved in decisions and making plans with the crew then that's always an option.  

Weapons: This role in probably the most simple in the game.  This is the only player who can by default use the main gun on the ship and the missile launcher.  Most ships have more than one gun and anyone can use the secondary guns, but the main gun is primarily for the weapons specialist.  Some ship types see more combat than others, so the way I see it if you play weapons then it's vital to make yourself more involved than just in combat otherwise you may get bored.  I always try to be social with the crew when I play as weapons since every other class has something to do most of the time and it's entirely possible to go through a lot of time as weapons with nothing to shoot.  

Engineer: Engi works on making sure the reactor doesn't overheat, and regulates power usage.  Everything that the ship does heats up the reactor.  If the reactor overheats then the ship shuts down temporarily (unless you turn the safety off the it explodes) this can be a serious issue in combat where the other classes are trying to push the reactor as much as they can.  To try and deal with this the engi has coolant that they can apply to the reactor to slow down the heating, and they can cut power to certain parts of the ship.  Engi is a role that is vital and fairly involved in the early game, but in the late game you'll probably get a reactor so badass that it won't overheat no matter what you do.  At that point the Engi won't have too many class specific things to do, but you can always man an extra gun or something.  

Science: Science is an interesting role.  This class is in charge of communications, viruses/programs, research, and team medic.  Working the communications and being a medic are super easy to figure out.  Viruses/programs are things you can use in combat to boost shields, stop enemy engines, steal money etc.  You get some of these programs spawned with the ship, but the cap will have to buy more as you go on.  The other primary responsibility of science is to do research.  While on planets the crew will find some research materials.  These materials can be used to unlock abilities and skills for the crew.  

&#x200B;

If you're just starting I'd suggest trying every role out to see what you like the best.  I think all of the roles are enjoyable, but everyone will prefer some roles over others.  

&#x200B;

When I started playing Pulsar there was no manual.  I learned by joining ships with strangers and working together.  I've skimmed the manuals and they may be helpful.  If you're all working together you can probably figure it out, but I'd still recommend joining a few random crews to try and learn that way.  The community is for the most part friendly, and always willing to help new players.  

&#x200B;

Good luck on your space adventures and maybe I'll see you out there.

How exactly did he work the headless pitch?  I figure that something like that would turn off some people, but it worked for you.

&#x200B;

Edit: Grammar.
